PKCS11-Proxy is a proxy for the PKCS11-library.

This project is based on a stripped down Gnome Keyring without all gnome
dependencies and other features.

The proxy tunnels PKCS11-requests over the network.  One possible use
is to store cryptograhic information on a seperate server.  This way
the crypto can be isolated from the rest of the system.

Example
=======

Here is an example of using pkcs11-proxy together with SoftHSM (from the
OpenDNSSEC project).  The benefit of this setup is that no extra hardware
is needed at all.  This could also be considered the greatest weakeness.
For demonstration purposes, however, security is not a consideration.

$ sudo adduser cgielen pkcs11
$ sudo adduser cgielen softhsm

$ softhsm --init-token --slot 0 --label test
The SO PIN must have a length between 4 and 255 characters.
Enter SO PIN:
The user PIN must have a length between 4 and 255 characters.
Enter user PIN:
The token has been initialized.

$ PKCS11_DAEMON_SOCKET="tcp://127.0.0.1:2345" pkcs11-daemon /usr/lib/libsofthsm.so
$ PKCS11_PROXY_SOCKET="tcp://127.0.0.1:2345" pkcs11-tool --module=/usr/lib/libpkcs11-proxy.so -L Available
slots: Slot 0           SoftHSM
  token label:   test token manuf:   SoftHSM token model:   SoftHSM
  token flags:   rng, login required, PIN initialized, token initialized,
  other flags=0x40 serial num  :  1


Version
=======

The version, with the git revision when built from a checkout, is printed by
pkcs11-daemon --version. The daemon logs it when it starts and the proxy
library logs it on C_Initialize when a log file is configured, so it is easy
to check what a deployment runs.

$ pkcs11-daemon --version
pkcs11-daemon 0.5.0 (v0.5.0-3-g1a2b3c4)


IPv6 and DNS
============

The PKCS11_DAEMON_SOCKET and PKCS11_PROXY_SOCKET environment variables can
have both hostnames and IPv6 addresses in them. getaddrinfo(3) is used to
resolve any DNS name.

$ PKCS11_DAEMON_SOCKET="tcp://server.example.com:2345" ...

If `server.example.com' resolves to more than one IP address (such as one
IPv4 and one IPv6 address), these will be tried sequentially. Currently,
no attempt is made to speed up connection establishment using Happy Eyeballs
(RFC 6555) or similar, so timeouts in case of unreachable addresses could
be expected to be quite problematic.

IPv6 addresses should be enclosed by square brackets.

$ PKCS11_DAEMON_SOCKET="tcp://[::1]:2345" ...


Encryption
==========

This version supports encrypting the communication between the client and
proxy using OpenSSL TLS-PSK (pre-shared key). The PSK is read from a file
that usees the GnuTLS psktool format. This format includes PSK identity
as well as key.

Currently, there is no way to specify the identity to use on the client
side (client tells server what identity should be used), and the first
identity found in the PSK file is used. The server side will correctly
look up the identity requested by the client in it's PSK file, so it is
possible to have one unique PSK identity and key per PKCS11 client, and
have all the identitys and keys in the PSK file for the PKCS11 daemon.

$ cat test.psk
test:e9622c85018998993fcc16f5ce9c15e9
$ PKCS11_PROXY_TLS_PSK_FILE="test.psk" \
  PKCS11_DAEMON_SOCKET="tls://server.example.com:2345" \
  pkcs11-daemon /usr/lib/libsofthsm.so
$ PKCS11_PROXY_TLS_PSK_FILE="test.psk" \
  PKCS11_PROXY_SOCKET="tls://server.example.com:2345" \
  pkcs11-tool --module=/usr/lib/libpkcs11-proxy.so -L Available
slots: Slot 0           SoftHSM
  token label:   test token manuf:   SoftHSM token model:   SoftHSM
  token flags:   rng, login required, PIN initialized, token initialized,
  other flags=0x40 serial num  :  1


Restricting client access
=========================

The daemon can restrict what clients are allowed to do with the PKCS11
module. This is useful when the module itself cannot separate key management
from key usage (SoftHSM for example) and the clients connecting through the
proxy should only be able to use the keys. The restrictions are enforced by
the daemon, so clients cannot bypass them by changing their own
configuration. The options are set in the daemon configuration file
referenced by PKCS11_PROXY_CONF_PATH.

$ cat /etc/pkcs11-proxy.conf
read_only_sessions = true
disabled_functions = C_WrapKey, C_DestroyObject

Read-only sessions
------------------

With read_only_sessions enabled, the daemon removes CKF_RW_SESSION from every
C_OpenSession request. The session opened in the module is a read-only
session and C_GetSessionInfo reports it as such. The daemon also reports the
token as write protected (CKF_WRITE_PROTECTED) with no read/write sessions
available in C_GetTokenInfo. Well behaved clients such as the Java SunPKCS11
provider then do not even request read/write sessions. Finally, C_InitToken,
C_InitPIN and C_SetPIN are rejected with CKR_TOKEN_WRITE_PROTECTED.

As a result token objects cannot be created, modified or destroyed through
the proxy and the module returns CKR_SESSION_READ_ONLY for such attempts.
Session objects (CKA_TOKEN = CK_FALSE) and all cryptographic operations keep
working. Note that using a key is a read operation, so this does not stop a
client from wrapping an extractable key with C_WrapKey. Use CKA_EXTRACTABLE
or CKA_WRAP_WITH_TRUSTED on the keys or disable C_WrapKey to prevent that.

Disabled functions
------------------

The disabled_functions option takes a comma separated list of PKCS11
function names. The daemon answers every call to a listed function with
CKR_FUNCTION_NOT_SUPPORTED without passing it to the module and logs the
rejected call. Any function can be listed except C_Initialize and C_Finalize,
which the proxy needs to operate. The daemon refuses to start when the list
contains an unknown name, so a typo cannot silently allow a call.

Disabling C_WrapKey prevents key export even for keys that have to stay
extractable for administrative purposes. The same applies to C_DestroyObject
and key deletion. Both complement read-only sessions, which only cover
modifications of token objects.
